er","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.265316,"lo":-76.579597,"d":49,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-16:45; Sa,Su 09:00-17:45","ad":"2400 East Fort Avenue","f":["wc","free"]},{"n":"Fort McHenry","g":"sights","k":"fort","la":39.263239,"lo":-76.579875,"d":196,"wd":"Q1438634","ad":"2400 East Fort Avenue","ds":"Fort McHenry is a historical American coastal pentagonal bastion fort on Locust Point, now a neighborhood of Baltimore, Maryland. It is best known for its role in the War of 1812, when it successfully defended Baltimore Harbor from an attack by the British Royal Navy from the Chesapeake Bay on September 13–14, 1814. The fort was built in 1798 and was used continuously by U.S.","wpu":"Fort McHenry","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/FortMcHenryAerialView.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"SS John W. Brown","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":39.267858,"lo":-76.569891,"d":926,"wp":"en:SS John W. Brown","wd":"Q1058850","ds":"SS John W. Brown is a Liberty ship, one of two still operational and one of three preserved as museum ships. As a Liberty ship, she operated as a merchant ship of the United States Merchant Marine during World War II and later was a vocational high school training ship in New York City for many years. Now preserved, she is a museum ship and cruise ship berthed at Pier 13 in Baltimore Harbor in Maryland.","wpu":"SS John W. Brown","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/SS_John_W_Brown.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Baltimore Immigration Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.272298,"lo":-76.593785,"d":1437,"w":"https://www.immigrationbaltimore.org/","ph":"+1-443-542-2263","ad":"1308 Beason Street"},{"n":"Professor Trash Wheel","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":39.27955,"lo":-76.58076,"d":1619,"w":"https://www.mrtrashwheel.com"},{"n":"Baltimore Museum of Industry","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.273756,"lo":-76.600998,"d":2053,"h":"We-Su 09:00-15:00","w":"https://www.thebmi.org/","wd":"Q4852896","ds":"The Baltimore Museum of Industry is a museum in Baltimore, Maryland, United States. Located in an old cannery complex, the museum has exhibits on various types of manufacturing and industry from the early 20th century. There are several hands-on sections with working equipment and other artifacts.","wpu":"Baltimore Museum of Industry","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/BaltimoreMuseumOfIndustry-FromQuay.jpg/960px-BaltimoreMuseumOfIndustry-FromQuay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Frederick-Douglass Maritime Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.279196,"lo":-76.596085,"d":2099,"w":"https://livingclassrooms.org/programs/fdimmp/"},{"n":"SS John Brown","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":39.259524,"lo":-76.556149,"d":2141,"w":"https://www.ssjohnwbrown.org/"},{"n":"The Preservation Society","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.282019,"lo":-76.591694,"d":2143,"w":"https://www.preservationsociety.com/"},{"n":"NS Savannah","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":39.258563,"lo":-76.555455,"d":2231,"w":"https://www.maritime.dot.gov/nssavannah","wp":"en:NS Savannah","wd":"Q1364576","ds":"NS Savannah was the first nuclear-powered merchant ship, launched on July 21, 1959, two years after the Soviet ice-breaker Lenin, the first nuclear-powered civilian vessel. A demonstration project for the potential peacetime uses of nuclear energy, she was built in the late 1950s at a cost of $46.9 million (including a $28.3 million nuclear reactor and fuel core).","wpu":"NS Savannah","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/NS_Savannah_En_Route_to_1962_World%27s_Fair_%2814698777183%29.jpg/960px-NS_Savannah_En_Route_to_1962_World%27s_Fair_%2814698777183%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Slavic Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":39.284386,"lo":-76.5